## 11. Reference Corpus Specification

### Target: 400+ labeled clauses across 9 categories

| Category | Target Count | Sources |
|----------|-------------|---------|
| Auto-renewal / subscription terms | ~45 | SaaS ToS (Spotify, Adobe, Netflix), FTC enforcement actions |
| Arbitration / dispute resolution / jury waiver | ~45 | Big tech ToS, consumer advocacy reports |
| Unilateral modification rights | ~45 | ToS;DR tagged clauses, social media ToS |
| Liability limitation / indemnification | ~45 | SaaS vendor agreements, freelancer contracts |
| Data collection / sharing / sale | ~45 | Privacy policies, data broker ToS, FTC actions |
| Termination conditions | ~45 | SaaS and employment agreements |
| Fee structures / hidden penalties | ~45 | Rental leases, SaaS billing terms |
| Governing law / venue | ~40 | Cross-jurisdictional agreements |
| Privacy / consent mechanisms | ~45 | GDPR-facing privacy policies, cookie policies |

### Risk Label Distribution (per category)

- ~40% `standard` — fair, balanced, industry-normal
- ~35% `cautionary` — legal but user-unfriendly
- ~25% `risky` — heavily one-sided, minimal notice, waives significant rights

### CSV Format (git-tracked)

```csv
text,category,risk_label,explanation,source
"Your subscription renews automatically at the end of each billing period. You may cancel at any time before the renewal date.",auto_renewal,standard,"Standard auto-renewal with unrestricted cancellation.",Spotify ToS 2024
```

### Corpus Build Strategy

1. Collect 15–20 real public documents (mix of big-company ToS, aggressive small-app ToS, HUD rental templates, FTC enforcement examples).
2. Run each through the Stage 2 segmenter to auto-split into candidate clauses.
3. Manually review and label each clause: `category`, `risk_label`, `explanation`, `source`.
4. Optionally use Gemini Flash to pre-label, then human-review. This is 10x faster than labeling from scratch.
5. Store as CSV, git-commit, then bulk-load into Postgres via `seed_corpus.py`.

